scrolling through social media it's not  hard to find misinformation about covit  but it is harder to spot a lie from  someone in a position of trust your  doctor they could bring particular harm  to the public and to individuals it's  very hard to quantify how much harm that  they could produce a global news  investigation found a web of physicians  mostly in ontario and b.c have been  sharing unverified information about  vaccine side effects specifically blood  clots and heart inflammation some even  claimed the shots killed children others  were accused of issuing falsified  medical exemptions for masks and  vaccines all of the doctors except one  have an active medical license on  wednesday ontario's health minister  christine elliott urged the province's  college of physicians and surgeons the  cpso to crack down  including reviewing the licenses of  physicians found to be spreading  misinformation  the cpso says it is it confirmed there  are more than 40 physicians under  investigation for spreading  misinformation on covet 19 and seven  have had their licenses restricted or  suspended politics professor wayne  petrozzi says elliott's call to action  though is meaningless  there's a limit to  to to how much they have to listen to  you in bc the college of physicians and  surgeons has refused to release the  number of complaints it has received in  relation to the pandemic they need to  put in place a much more robust  framework of openness and transparency  with respect to  not only the the activities of the  associations but the decisions they make  health and law policy expert trudeau  lemons says historically these agencies  have not been proactive but notes  imposing too much government oversight  could also be problematic an excessive  level of policing that for example would  lead them to interfere  with normal debate within the medical  community a fine line experts agree  needs adjustment the status quo they say  leaves patients and the public  vulnerable jamie morocco global news  toronto
 

